```cshtml
@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "Ignoring Files - Git";
}

<main id="tutorials" class="container py-1">
    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- Sidebar Navigation -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- Reading Content Area -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">

            <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
                <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

                    <!-- HEADER -->
                    <header class="mb-4">
                        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">Ignoring Files with .gitignore</h2>

                        <p class="lead text-muted">
                            Not every file in your project should be tracked by Git.
                            Files such as build output, temporary files, logs, and local
                            configuration files often do not belong in version control.
                            Git allows you to exclude these files using a
                            <code>.gitignore</code> file.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            In this lesson, you will learn how to create a
                            <code>.gitignore</code> file, define ignore patterns,
                            and verify which files Git tracks.
                        </p>
                    </header>

                    <!-- SECTION 1 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">1. What Is .gitignore?</h4>

                        <p>
                            A <code>.gitignore</code> file contains patterns that tell Git
                            which untracked files and directories it should ignore.
                            Git uses these patterns when identifying files to include
                            in version control.
                        </p>

                        <p>Common examples of files to ignore include:</p>

                        <ul>
                            <li>Temporary files and application logs.</li>
                            <li>Build output and generated files.</li>
                            <li>IDE-specific configuration files.</li>
                            <li>Local environment configuration files.</li>
                            <li>Dependency folders that can be restored from package managers.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">
                            <strong>Important:</strong>
                            <code>.gitignore</code> does not automatically stop tracking
                            files that have already been committed. It primarily affects
                            untracked files.
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SECTION 2 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">2. Create a .gitignore File</h4>

                        <p>
                            Create a file named <code>.gitignore</code> in the root
                            directory of your Git repository.
                        </p>

                        <p>For example, your project structure might look like this:</p>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            my-project/<br />
                            ├── src/<br />
                            ├── tests/<br />
                            ├── bin/<br />
                            ├── obj/<br />
                            ├── app.log<br />
                            └── .gitignore
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            Add patterns for files and folders that should not be tracked.
                        </p>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            # Build output<br />
                            bin/<br />
                            obj/<br />
                            <br />
                            # Log files<br />
                            *.log<br />
                            <br />
                            # Temporary files<br />
                            *.tmp<br />
                            <br />
                            # Environment configuration<br />
                            .env
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            Lines beginning with <code>#</code> are comments.
                            Each remaining pattern tells Git which matching untracked
                            files or directories to ignore.
                        </p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SECTION 3 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">3. Common .gitignore Patterns</h4>

                        <div class="table-responsive">
                            <table class="table table-bordered table-hover align-middle">
                                <thead class="table-light">
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Pattern</th>
                                        <th>Meaning</th>
                                        <th>Example</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>*.log</code></td>
                                        <td>Matches files ending in .log.</td>
                                        <td><code>app.log</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>temp/</code></td>
                                        <td>Ignores a directory named temp.</td>
                                        <td><code>temp/cache.txt</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>*.tmp</code></td>
                                        <td>Matches files ending in .tmp.</td>
                                        <td><code>debug.tmp</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>/config.json</code></td>
                                        <td>Matches config.json at the .gitignore file's directory level.</td>
                                        <td><code>config.json</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>!important.log</code></td>
                                        <td>Negates an earlier matching ignore pattern, subject to parent-directory rules.</td>
                                        <td><code>important.log</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>.env</code></td>
                                        <td>Ignores matching .env files according to the pattern's scope.</td>
                                        <td><code>.env</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SECTION 4 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">4. Verify Ignored Files</h4>

                        <p>
                            After adding patterns to <code>.gitignore</code>, use
                            <code>git status</code> to check which changes Git reports.
                        </p>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git status
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            To identify the exact ignore rule that matches a file,
                            use <code>git check-ignore -v</code>.
                        </p>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git check-ignore -v app.log
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            If the file matches an ignore rule, Git displays the
                            rule's source and the matching path.
                        </p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SECTION 5 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">
                            5. Stop Tracking an Already Tracked File
                        </h4>

                        <p>
                            If a file was already committed, adding it to
                            <code>.gitignore</code> will not remove it from version control.
                            To stop tracking the file while keeping it on your computer,
                            use <code>git rm --cached</code>.
                        </p>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span>
                            git rm --cached config.json
                        </div>

                        <p>
                            Then commit the change:
                        </p>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white rounded p-3 mb-3 font-monospace">
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span>
                            git add .gitignore<br />
                            <span class="text-secondary">$</span>
                            git commit -m "Stop tracking config file"
                        </div>

                        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0">
                            <strong>Security note:</strong>
                            If a secret or password has already been committed or pushed,
                            ignoring the file is not enough. Remove the secret from the
                            relevant history when necessary and rotate the exposed
                            credential.
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SECTION 6 -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">6. Practical Workflow</h4>

                        <ol>
                            <li class="mb-2">Create a <code>.gitignore</code> file in your repository.</li>
                            <li class="mb-2">Add patterns for temporary files, logs, and build output.</li>
                            <li class="mb-2">Run <code>git status</code> to review the changes.</li>
                            <li class="mb-2">Use <code>git check-ignore -v filename</code> to inspect matching rules.</li>
                            <li>Commit the <code>.gitignore</code> file so the team shares the same ignore rules.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </section>

                    <!-- SUMMARY -->
                    <section class="mb-4">
                        <div class="alert alert-dark border-0 mb-0">
                            <h5 class="fw-bold mb-2">
                                <i class="bi bi-lightbulb me-2"></i>
                                Key Takeaway
                            </h5>

                            <p class="mb-0">
                                The <code>.gitignore</code> file helps keep generated,
                                temporary, and local-only files out of your Git repository.
                                Use clear patterns, verify them with
                                <code>git check-ignore</code>, and remember that ignoring
                                a file does not remove it from Git if it is already tracked.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <!-- PREVIOUS / NEXT NAVIGATION -->
                    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center border-top pt-4 mt-4">

                        <!-- PREVIOUS TOPIC -->
                        <a href="@Url.Action(
                                    "InspectingHistory",
                                    "LocalGitWorkflow",
                                    new { area = "GitHub" }
                                 )"
                           class="btn btn-outline-secondary px-3">

                            <span class="me-2">←</span>
                            Previous: Inspecting History

                        </a>

                        <!-- NEXT TOPIC -->
                        <a href="@Url.Action(
                                    "GitBranchingConcept",
                                    "BranchingAndMerging",
                                    new { area = "GitHub" }
                                 )"
                           class="btn btn-primary px-3">

                            Next: Git Branching Concepts
                            <span class="ms-2">→</span>

                        </a>

                    </div>

                </div>
            </div>

        </section>
    </div>
</main>
```